  "question": {
    "text": "Let W denote the words in the English dictionary. Define the relation R by :\n$R=\\{(x, y) \\in W \\times W \\mid$ the words $x$ and $y$ have at least one letter in common $\\}$. Then $R$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "qQp7I",
      "text": "\nnot reflexive, symmetric and transitive\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "bjqKd",
      "text": "\nreflexive, symmetric and not transitive\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "PDu9X",
      "text": "\nreflexive, symmetric and transitive\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "b6QqR",
      "text": "\nreflexive, not symmetric and transitive",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Clearly $(\\mathrm{x}, \\mathrm{x}) \\in \\mathrm{R} \\forall \\mathrm{x} \\in \\mathrm{W}$. So, $\\mathrm{R}$ is reflexive.\nLet $(x, y) \\in R$, then $(y, x) \\in R$ as $x$ and $y$ have at least one letter in common. So, $R$ is symmetric.\nBut $R$ is not transitive for example\nLet $x=$ DELHI, $y=$ DWARKA and $z=$ PARK\nthen $(x, y) \\in R$ and $(y, z) \\in R$ but $(x, z) \\notin R$.",
    "image": null
  },
